\newlabel{def:D}{{1}{5}{Placement radius}{definition.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The manipulation check, at the level of single trials.} Left: what changed --- the fifty starting positions of the target, shipped and repaired, inside the $5\times 5$\,cm region the task file declares. Right: what happened, one column per trial index, green for success. The test is paired, so the raw outcomes are shown rather than two bar heights: a column green above and red below is a trial the repair cost. \textbf  {The middle block is the control}: handed the true target once at reset, the same controller succeeds on every one of the thirty repaired states the lookup constant fails on, so those states are not harder --- only the constant is wrong on them. Its shipped row is $n{=}10$ (dotted cells were not run); every other cell is $n{=}30$. Generated by \texttt  {paper/render\_fig15\_e1.py} from \texttt  {results/e1\_shipped\_vs\_repaired.json} and \texttt  {results/e5\_trials.json}.}}{6}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e1}{{4}{6}{\textbf {The manipulation check, at the level of single trials.} Left: what changed --- the fifty starting positions of the target, shipped and repaired, inside the $5\times 5$\,cm region the task file declares. Right: what happened, one column per trial index, green for success. The test is paired, so the raw outcomes are shown rather than two bar heights: a column green above and red below is a trial the repair cost. \textbf {The middle block is the control}: handed the true target once at reset, the same controller succeeds on every one of the thirty repaired states the lookup constant fails on, so those states are not harder --- only the constant is wrong on them. Its shipped row is $n{=}10$ (dotted cells were not run); every other cell is $n{=}30$. Generated by \texttt {paper/render\_fig15\_e1.py} from \texttt {results/e1\_shipped\_vs\_repaired.json} and \texttt {results/e5\_trials.json}}{figure.caption.14}{}}
\newlabel{def:H}{{2}{6}{Lookup-admissibility}{definition.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Which objects, and why it matters.}{6}{section*.21}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Sampling the region the benchmark declares costs a lookup policy half its score.} Task $0$, $n{=}30$ per cell, exact McNemar over matched trial indices --- one variable changes, the fifty initial states. The constant is bit-identical to the shipped target position, so against a target that actually moves it should degrade, and it does: $0.833 \to 0.333$, $p = 0.0007$. \textbf  {This is the manipulation check for \S  \ref {sec:declared}}: it establishes that the shipped states cannot defeat a table lookup and the repaired ones can, so the defect is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. \textbf  {The control row closes the obvious hole}: handed the true target once at reset, the same controller succeeds on \emph  {every one} of the thirty repaired states, so those states are not harder --- only the constant breaks on them. That the manipulation reached the \emph  {simulator} and not merely the files is checked there too: at step $0$ the physics engine placed the object at $30$ distinct positions spanning $4.8\times 4.7$\,cm under the repair and at one position, to nine decimals, under the shipped states (\texttt  {results/e5\_object\_positions.json}). The trained head drops in the same direction, $0.633 \to 0.433$, which this $n$ does not resolve ($p = 0.18$) --- and which we would not read as exploitation even if it did, because the two explanations are not separated here: the head was fit on this suite's own demonstrations, so a drop on states drawn from anywhere else is as consistent with plain distribution shift as with losing a memorised placement. Only the lookup arm, whose constant we wrote down ourselves, admits the second reading unambiguously. Drawn per trial in Figure~\ref {fig:e1}; artifact \texttt  {results/e1\_shipped\_vs\_repaired.json}.}}{7}{table.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:e1}{{4}{7}{\textbf {Sampling the region the benchmark declares costs a lookup policy half its score.} Task $0$, $n{=}30$ per cell, exact McNemar over matched trial indices --- one variable changes, the fifty initial states. The constant is bit-identical to the shipped target position, so against a target that actually moves it should degrade, and it does: $0.833 \to 0.333$, $p = 0.0007$. \textbf {This is the manipulation check for \S \ref {sec:declared}}: it establishes that the shipped states cannot defeat a table lookup and the repaired ones can, so the defect is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. \textbf {The control row closes the obvious hole}: handed the true target once at reset, the same controller succeeds on \emph {every one} of the thirty repaired states, so those states are not harder --- only the constant breaks on them. That the manipulation reached the \emph {simulator} and not merely the files is checked there too: at step $0$ the physics engine placed the object at $30$ distinct positions spanning $4.8\times 4.7$\,cm under the repair and at one position, to nine decimals, under the shipped states (\texttt {results/e5\_object\_positions.json}). The trained head drops in the same direction, $0.633 \to 0.433$, which this $n$ does not resolve ($p = 0.18$) --- and which we would not read as exploitation even if it did, because the two explanations are not separated here: the head was fit on this suite's own demonstrations, so a drop on states drawn from anywhere else is as consistent with plain distribution shift as with losing a memorised placement. Only the lookup arm, whose constant we wrote down ourselves, admits the second reading unambiguously. Drawn per trial in Figure~\ref {fig:e1}; artifact \texttt {results/e1\_shipped\_vs\_repaired.json}}{table.caption.15}{}}
