\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The same comparison at two units of analysis. Trials within a task share an object, a scene, a goal supply and a near-deterministic trajectory, so the trial-level test is anti-conservative. \textbf  {The task-level tests do not show equivalence --- they show this design cannot resolve the question in either direction.} With eight ties the sign test has two informative units and its smallest attainable $p$ is $0.5$; the permutation test has four arrangements and the same floor. Reading $p = 1.0$ as ``no difference'' would repeat, in the other direction, the error this section withdraws. The design effect ($\approx 8$, bootstrap $[5.4, 10.0]$, estimated from one non-degenerate cluster) is reported as a description of the clustering, not as a correction to any $p$. Artifact: \texttt  {results/suite\_inference.json}.}}{12}{table.caption.39}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:fullsuite}{{9}{12}{The same comparison at two units of analysis. Trials within a task share an object, a scene, a goal supply and a near-deterministic trajectory, so the trial-level test is anti-conservative. \textbf {The task-level tests do not show equivalence --- they show this design cannot resolve the question in either direction.} With eight ties the sign test has two informative units and its smallest attainable $p$ is $0.5$; the permutation test has four arrangements and the same floor. Reading $p = 1.0$ as ``no difference'' would repeat, in the other direction, the error this section withdraws. The design effect ($\approx 8$, bootstrap $[5.4, 10.0]$, estimated from one non-degenerate cluster) is reported as a description of the clustering, not as a correction to any $p$. Artifact: \texttt {results/suite\_inference.json}}{table.caption.39}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The goal supply does not explain the outcome.} Four tasks receive the same constant to within half a millimetre in all three axes and score $0$, $0$, $0$ and $10$ out of ten. This is a deterministic contrast, not a statistical one. We previously also offered a correlation between placement radius and success ($r = +0.52$) as evidence here; \textbf  {we withdraw it}. Its exact permutation $p$ is $0.13$ and deleting task $3$ flips it to $-0.25$ --- the sign is one task, the error this section reports. Artifact: \texttt  {results/constant\_attribution.json}.}}{12}{table.caption.41}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:attrib}{{10}{12}{\textbf {The goal supply does not explain the outcome.} Four tasks receive the same constant to within half a millimetre in all three axes and score $0$, $0$, $0$ and $10$ out of ten. This is a deterministic contrast, not a statistical one. We previously also offered a correlation between placement radius and success ($r = +0.52$) as evidence here; \textbf {we withdraw it}. Its exact permutation $p$ is $0.13$ and deleting task $3$ flips it to $-0.25$ --- the sign is one task, the error this section reports. Artifact: \texttt {results/constant\_attribution.json}}{table.caption.41}{}}
